"""Author-written generic paragraphs for C15-R2 (fresh; written 2026-10-07 before any model run; D74).

Diverse everyday / encyclopaedic topics, free of logic, validity, verification and judgement vocabulary and of
the R2 concept words. No sentence is reused from the A-stage paragraphs (unit test T2: no shared 8-word
sequence). 126 main paragraphs (split 63/63 into select2/confirm2 by seed 9500) + 6 SMOKE2 paragraphs.
Each yields >= 64 tokens in all three tokenizers (checked in make_splits2).
"""

PARAGRAPHS = [
    # nature and geography
    "Along the northern edge of the marsh, a narrow wooden boardwalk winds between tall reeds and shallow pools of dark water. In early morning the air is filled with the calls of wading birds, and herons stand motionless at the water's edge, waiting for fish. Dragonflies hover above the surface in the warmer months, while in winter the pools freeze over and the reeds turn a pale gold colour that glows in the low sunlight.",
    "The plateau rises sharply from the surrounding plains, and its flat top is covered with short grass and scattered boulders. Strong winds blow across it almost every day, bending the few stunted trees that manage to grow there. Shepherds bring their flocks up in early summer and stay until the first frosts, living in simple stone huts. From the edge of the cliffs on a clear day, the view stretches for more than a hundred kilometres.",
    "A small spring rises at the bottom of the hill, where cold water bubbles up through a bed of pale sand. For centuries the people of the nearby village came here to fill their jugs, and a low stone wall was built around the pool to keep animals away. Today the water still flows, feeding a narrow stream that runs through meadows full of wild flowers before it joins the larger river in the valley below.",
    "In the dry season the riverbed turns into a wide ribbon of cracked mud and pebbles, and only a few stagnant pools remain. Animals travel long distances to drink from them, and the banks become crowded with tracks of every size. When the rains finally return, the water rises within hours, sweeping branches and debris downstream, and within a few weeks the surrounding plain is green again and loud with frogs and insects.",
    "The coastline here is made of tall chalk cliffs that crumble a little more every winter. Storms tear away large sections, leaving fresh white scars that slowly weather to grey. Fossils of ancient sea creatures are often found among the fallen blocks on the beach, and collectors come from far away to search for them at low tide. Walkers are warned to keep well back from the edge, where the ground can give way without warning.",
    "High in the mountains, the glacier moves so slowly that its motion cannot be seen, yet it carries rocks the size of houses down the valley over many centuries. Deep cracks open in its surface during the summer, and meltwater rushes through hidden tunnels beneath the ice. Scientists have been measuring its length for more than a hundred years, and their records show that it has been shrinking steadily for most of that time.",
    "The heath is a wide open landscape of heather, gorse and sandy soil, shaped by grazing and occasional fires. In late summer the heather flowers turn the hills purple, and the air hums with bees collecting nectar. Lizards bask on warm patches of bare sand, and skylarks rise singing high above the ground. Without regular grazing, the heath would slowly turn into woodland, so wardens manage it carefully to keep it open.",
    "Mangrove forests grow along warm tropical coasts where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots rise out of the mud like stilts, trapping sediment and protecting the shore from waves and storms. Young fish shelter among the roots before moving out to open water, and crabs climb up and down the trunks with the tides. Many coastal communities depend on these forests for food, firewood and protection from the sea.",
    "The canyon was carved by a single river over millions of years, cutting down through layer after layer of coloured rock. Standing on the rim, visitors can see bands of red, orange and cream stretching away into the distance, each layer representing a different period of the planet's history. At the bottom, far below, the river looks like a thin silver thread, although in reality it is wide, fast and dangerous to cross.",
    "Fog rolls in from the sea almost every evening during the summer, creeping up the valleys and covering the hills in a thick white blanket. The trees along the ridge have adapted by catching droplets of water on their needles, which then drip to the ground and keep the soil moist through months without rain. By late morning the fog usually burns away, revealing a bright blue sky and a sparkling sea below.",
    "The oasis appears suddenly in the middle of the desert, a cluster of date palms surrounding a pool of clear water fed by an underground spring. For travellers crossing the sand on foot, it was once a vital stopping point, and traders would rest there for days before continuing their journey. Today a small town has grown around it, with narrow streets, a busy market and gardens irrigated by channels dug many generations ago.",
    "On the high moor, the weather can change from bright sunshine to driving rain in less than an hour. Peat bogs cover much of the ground, and walkers must follow the marked paths to avoid sinking into the soft, wet earth. The bogs store enormous amounts of carbon and water, and they preserve objects remarkably well. Wooden tools and even ancient bodies have been found in them, almost perfectly intact after thousands of years.",
    "Spring arrives late in the far north, but when it comes, it comes quickly. The snow melts within a few weeks, rivers swell with icy water, and the ground thaws enough for plants to push up through the soil. Migrating birds return in huge numbers to nest on the open tundra, and the days grow longer until, by midsummer, the sun barely sets at all and the night is little more than a long golden evening.",
    "The volcanic island has black sand beaches and steep slopes covered in dense green forest. Its last eruption was more than a century ago, but steam still rises from vents near the summit, and hot springs bubble up along the coast. Farmers grow coffee and fruit in the rich soil on the lower slopes, and fishermen work the waters around the island, where the sea floor drops away steeply into very deep water.",
    "Rivers that flow through limestone country often disappear underground, vanishing into sinkholes and reappearing kilometres away as powerful springs. Between those points they run through dark caves and tunnels, some of which have never been fully explored. Cave divers who follow these underground rivers face cold water, narrow passages and total darkness, and they map each new section carefully so that others can follow them safely in the future.",
    # history
    "The old trading port was once one of the busiest harbours on the coast, with ships arriving from distant lands carrying spices, silk and timber. Merchants built grand houses along the waterfront, and their warehouses stretched for streets behind them. When the river mouth slowly filled with silt, larger ships could no longer reach the docks, and trade moved elsewhere. Today the port is a quiet town, but the old merchant houses still stand.",
    "In the middle ages, the town was surrounded by a thick stone wall with four gates, each closed at sunset and opened again at dawn. Travellers who arrived late had to wait outside until morning, sometimes sleeping in inns built just beyond the walls. The gates were guarded day and night, and anyone bringing goods into the town had to pay a small tax. Parts of the wall still survive today, along with one of the gates.",
    "The first printing presses changed the way knowledge spread across the continent. Before them, books had to be copied by hand, a slow and expensive process that kept them in the hands of a few wealthy readers. Within a few decades of the new invention, thousands of titles were being produced, prices fell sharply, and ideas could travel from one city to another far more quickly than ever before, reaching people who had never owned a book.",
    "During the great age of canal building, thousands of workers dug long waterways across the countryside by hand, using picks and wheelbarrows. The canals allowed heavy goods such as coal, stone and grain to be moved cheaply on barges pulled by horses walking along the towpath. For several decades they were the backbone of the country's trade, until the arrival of the railways made them slow and old-fashioned by comparison.",
    "The ancient city was built on a hill overlooking a fertile plain, and at its height it was home to tens of thousands of people. Archaeologists have uncovered streets paved with stone, public baths, a large theatre and the remains of houses decorated with colourful wall paintings. The city was abandoned after a series of earthquakes and wars, and over time its ruins were buried beneath soil and forgotten for more than a thousand years.",
    "Long before modern clocks, people measured time using the sun, the stars and simple devices such as sundials and water clocks. Monasteries rang bells at fixed hours to call monks to prayer, and towns gradually adopted similar systems for markets and work. The invention of mechanical clocks with gears and weights allowed time to be measured more precisely, and soon large clocks were installed in church towers and town halls across the land.",
    "The railway reached the small market town in the middle of the nineteenth century, and within a few years its character had changed completely. Farmers could now send fresh milk and vegetables to the city overnight, and factories opened near the station to take advantage of the new connection. Visitors began to arrive on day trips, and several hotels were built to receive them, along with a promenade and a small public garden.",
    "In the years after the great fire, the city was rebuilt almost entirely in stone and tile instead of wood. New laws required wider streets and fixed the height of buildings, and a team of architects drew up plans for squares, avenues and public buildings. Many of the churches and halls designed at that time are still standing, and the fire is remembered every year with a small ceremony near the place where it began.",
    "For centuries, the mountain pass was the only practical route between the two valleys, and it was used by merchants, soldiers and pilgrims alike. In winter it was often blocked by deep snow, and travellers caught in storms sometimes lost their lives. A small hospice was built at the top by monks who offered shelter and food to anyone who needed it. The building still stands and welcomes walkers in summer.",
    "The first public libraries opened in many towns during the nineteenth century, often funded by wealthy donors who believed that reading could improve people's lives. Workers who had never been able to afford books could now borrow them for free, and reading rooms were filled every evening with people studying newspapers, learning new trades or simply enjoying stories. Many of these libraries still occupy the handsome buildings constructed for them at the time.",
    "Before the age of steam, the windmill was one of the most important machines in the countryside. Its great wooden arms turned heavy stones that ground grain into flour, and in low-lying areas windmills were also used to pump water out of the fields. Millers were important figures in village life, and the mill itself was often the tallest building for miles around. A few traditional mills are still working today, mostly as museums.",
    "The old silk road was not a single road but a vast network of routes linking the east and the west across deserts, mountains and grasslands. Caravans carried silk, porcelain, spices and precious metals, while ideas, religions and inventions travelled along the same paths. Few merchants ever made the whole journey; instead goods passed from hand to hand through many markets, increasing in price at every stage of the long trip.",
    # science
    "Light travels so fast that it could circle the planet more than seven times in a single second, yet even at that speed it takes about eight minutes to reach us from the sun. When we look at distant stars, we see them not as they are now but as they were when the light left them, sometimes thousands or even millions of years ago. Astronomers use this delay to study the early history of the universe.",
    "Every living cell contains a set of instructions written in a chemical code. These instructions tell the cell which proteins to build, when to divide and how to respond to its surroundings. Although the code uses only four basic letters, the sequences are extremely long, and tiny changes can have large effects. Scientists can now read these sequences quickly and cheaply, which has transformed medicine, agriculture and the study of evolution.",
    "When warm moist air rises, it cools, and the water vapour it carries condenses into tiny droplets that form clouds. If the droplets grow large enough, they fall as rain, and in very cold conditions they may freeze into snowflakes or hailstones. The shape and height of clouds can tell a careful observer a great deal about the weather to come, which is why sailors and farmers have always paid close attention to the sky.",
    "Origami, the art of folding paper, turns a single flat square into birds, flowers, boxes and animals without any cutting or glue. A few basic folds, repeated and combined in clever ways, can produce figures of astonishing complexity. Mathematicians have studied the rules behind these folds, and engineers have used the same ideas to design solar panels that unfold in space and medical devices small enough to travel through blood vessels.",
    "Plants make their own food using sunlight, water and carbon dioxide from the air. Inside their leaves, special structures capture light energy and use it to build sugars, releasing oxygen as a by-product. Almost all life on the planet depends directly or indirectly on this process, since animals eat plants or eat other animals that do. The oxygen we breathe was produced over billions of years by plants and tiny ocean organisms.",
    "Sound is a vibration that travels through the air as a series of pressure waves. When the waves reach our ears, they make a thin membrane vibrate, and tiny bones pass these movements to the inner ear, where they are turned into signals for the brain. Sound travels faster through water than through air, and faster still through solid materials, which is why you can hear an approaching train by putting your ear to the rail.",
    "The moon has no atmosphere and no liquid water, so the footprints left by the first astronauts may remain undisturbed for millions of years. Its surface is covered with craters made by impacts from rocks and dust drifting through space. Because the same side of the moon always faces the earth, people never saw the far side until spacecraft travelled around it and sent back photographs in the twentieth century.",
    "Bacteria are tiny single-celled organisms that live almost everywhere, from hot springs and deep ocean vents to the soil in our gardens and the inside of our own bodies. Most of them are harmless, and many are extremely useful, helping us digest food, breaking down dead material and making nutrients available to plants. Only a small number cause disease, and modern medicine has developed many ways to fight them.",
    "Earthquakes release energy that travels through the ground as waves, and instruments called seismometers can detect these waves even on the other side of the planet. By comparing the times at which waves arrive at different stations, scientists can work out where an earthquake started and how deep it was. The same method has revealed the structure of the planet's interior, including its solid inner core and liquid outer core.",
    "Rainbows appear when sunlight passes through raindrops and is bent and reflected inside them. Each colour of light bends by a slightly different amount, so the white light of the sun is spread out into a band of colours. To see a rainbow, you must stand with the sun behind you and rain in front of you. Sometimes a second, fainter rainbow appears above the first, with its colours in the reverse order.",
    "The human brain contains tens of billions of nerve cells, each connected to thousands of others. Signals pass between them as tiny electrical pulses and chemical messages, and patterns of activity across these networks give rise to thought, memory and movement. Although scientists have learned a great deal about how individual cells work, how they combine to produce experience and behaviour remains one of the great open questions of science.",
    "Ice is less dense than liquid water, which is why it floats. This unusual property has important consequences for life on the planet. When a lake freezes in winter, the ice forms a layer on top that insulates the water below, allowing fish and other creatures to survive until spring. If ice sank instead, lakes and seas could freeze solid from the bottom up, and much of the life in them would perish.",
    "Comets are lumps of ice, dust and rock that travel around the sun on long, stretched orbits. When a comet comes close to the sun, the heat turns some of its ice into gas, which streams away to form a glowing tail that can stretch for millions of kilometres. Some comets return every few years, while others take thousands of years to complete a single orbit and may be seen only once in human history.",
    "Seeds can survive for remarkable lengths of time when they are kept cool and dry. Some have sprouted after being buried for hundreds of years, and scientists once grew a plant from a seed found in an ancient storage jar. To protect the variety of crops grown around the world, special seed vaults now store millions of samples deep underground, where they can be kept safe from war, disaster and climate change.",
    "Fireflies, some deep-sea fish and certain fungi produce their own light through a chemical reaction inside their bodies. This light is usually cold, wasting very little energy as heat. Creatures use it to attract mates, to lure prey or to confuse predators. In parts of the ocean where sunlight never reaches, a large share of the animals produce light, and the darkness is often dotted with tiny flashes and glowing patterns.",
    "Tides are caused mainly by the pull of the moon on the oceans, with a smaller contribution from the sun. As the planet rotates, most coastlines experience two high tides and two low tides each day. When the sun and moon line up, their pulls combine to produce especially high and low tides, known as spring tides. In some narrow bays the difference between high and low water can exceed fifteen metres.",
    # technology
    "The first computers filled entire rooms and used thousands of glass valves that frequently burned out. They were programmed by setting switches and plugging cables into large panels, and a single calculation could take hours to prepare. Over the following decades, the valves were replaced by transistors and then by tiny integrated circuits, and machines became smaller, cheaper and vastly more powerful, until millions of people carried one in their pockets every day.",
    "Modern bridges are designed to bend slightly in strong winds and to expand and contract as the temperature changes. Engineers build in special joints that allow the deck to move without cracking, and long suspension bridges hang from steel cables strong enough to carry enormous loads. Before construction begins, scale models are often tested in wind tunnels to make sure that the structure will remain stable even during the most violent storms.",
    "Solar panels turn sunlight directly into electricity using thin layers of specially treated material. When light strikes the panel, it knocks electrons loose, creating a small electric current. A single panel produces only a modest amount of power, but large fields of panels can supply whole towns. As the cost of making them has fallen dramatically, solar power has become one of the fastest growing sources of energy in the world.",
    "Satellites orbiting high above the planet send signals that allow receivers on the ground to calculate their position with great accuracy. Each satellite carries an extremely precise clock, and by measuring tiny differences in the time it takes signals from several satellites to arrive, a receiver can work out exactly where it is. This technology now guides ships, aircraft, delivery vans and hikers, and it is used in farming and mapping as well.",
    "Refrigeration changed the way people eat more than almost any other invention of the last two centuries. Before it, food had to be eaten quickly, salted, dried or smoked to stop it from spoiling. Ice was cut from frozen lakes in winter and stored in insulated houses for use in summer. Today chilled lorries carry fresh produce across continents, and most homes keep food fresh for days or weeks in a cold box.",
    "A lock on a canal or river allows boats to move between stretches of water at different heights. The boat enters a chamber with gates at each end; the gates behind it close, and water is either let in or drained out until the level matches the next stretch. Then the gates in front open and the boat continues. The process is slow but simple, and some locks have been working for centuries.",
    "Early photographs required the subject to sit perfectly still for several minutes while the image formed on a treated glass or metal sheet. People often look stern in old portraits simply because holding a smile for so long was difficult. Over time, chemical processes became faster and more sensitive, and by the end of the nineteenth century ordinary people could take snapshots of their families, holidays and everyday lives.",
    "In some coastal regions, salt is still made the traditional way, by letting sea water evaporate in shallow clay pans under the summer sun. Workers move the water from pond to pond as it becomes saltier, and finally rake the white crystals into small heaps along the edges. The work is slow and depends entirely on the weather, since a single heavy rainstorm can dissolve weeks of careful labour in a few hours.",
    "Water treatment plants clean river or lake water before it reaches our taps. The water passes through screens that remove large debris, then settles in tanks where particles sink to the bottom. It is filtered through layers of sand and gravel and finally disinfected to kill harmful microbes. Engineers test samples throughout the day, and the whole system runs continuously so that clean water is available at any hour.",
    "The invention of the elevator made tall buildings practical. Before it, few people wanted to climb more than five or six flights of stairs, so most buildings stayed low. Once safe passenger lifts with automatic brakes became available, architects began to design towers with dozens of floors, and city skylines changed dramatically. Today the fastest lifts travel at more than sixty kilometres per hour, carrying people to the top of skyscrapers in under a minute.",
    # everyday life
    "On Saturday mornings the market square fills with stalls selling fresh vegetables, flowers, cheese and home-made bread. Farmers arrive before dawn to set up their tables, and by eight o'clock the first shoppers are wandering between the stalls with baskets on their arms. Neighbours stop to chat, children are given samples of honey and fruit, and a musician usually plays near the fountain, hoping for a little money in his open case.",
    "Moving to a new house always takes longer than expected. There are boxes to pack and label, furniture to take apart, and countless small decisions about what to keep and what to give away. On the day itself, everything seems to happen at once, and by evening the new rooms are full of half-opened boxes. It usually takes weeks before the place begins to feel like home and everything finds its proper place.",
    "Every morning the old man walks his dog along the river path, rain or shine. He knows most of the other regular walkers by name, and they stop for a few words about the weather, the state of the path or the ducks on the water. In autumn he collects chestnuts along the way, and in summer he sometimes sits on a bench for an hour watching the boats go by before heading home for lunch.",
    "Learning to ride a bicycle is a rite of passage for many children. At first there are wobbles and falls, scraped knees and moments of frustration, while a parent runs alongside holding the back of the seat. Then, almost suddenly, the child finds the balance and pedals off alone, often without realising that the hand has let go. Few people forget the feeling of freedom that comes with that first successful ride.",
    "The library in our neighbourhood is open late on Thursdays, and it is often busier in the evening than during the day. Students come to study in the quiet reading room, retired people browse the newspapers, and parents bring their children to choose picture books. A small group meets in the back room every week to talk about a novel they have all read, and their discussions sometimes become quite lively.",
    "Gardening teaches patience. Seeds planted in spring may take weeks to appear, and some plants need years before they flower or bear fruit. The weather does not always cooperate, slugs and birds help themselves to the crops, and weeds seem to grow faster than anything else. Yet there is great pleasure in watching a bare patch of earth turn green and in picking vegetables that you have grown yourself.",
    "When the snow falls heavily overnight, the whole town wakes up to a different world. Cars are buried under white mounds, schools close for the day and the streets are strangely quiet. Children run outside to build figures and have snowball fights, while their parents dig paths to the front door. By the next afternoon the roads are usually clear again, and the snow on the pavements has turned to grey slush.",
    "The café on the corner opens at seven, and by half past seven there is already a queue of people on their way to work. The owner knows most of them and starts preparing their usual orders as soon as they walk in. In the afternoons the atmosphere changes, with students working at the tables and friends meeting for long conversations, while the smell of fresh pastries drifts out through the open door onto the street.",
    "Doing the laundry used to take a whole day. Water had to be heated over a fire, clothes were scrubbed by hand on a ridged board, and everything was wrung out and hung on lines to dry. The arrival of electric washing machines transformed this work, freeing many hours each week, mostly for women, who had traditionally done it. Today a full load can be washed and dried with the press of a few buttons.",
    "Family photo albums are full of moments that seemed ordinary at the time: a birthday party, a day at the beach, a first day at school. Looking through them years later, people notice details they had forgotten, such as the pattern of a wallpaper, an old car or the hairstyle of a relative. The pictures become a record not only of the family but also of how everyday life has changed over the decades.",
    "Public parks give city dwellers a place to escape the noise and crowds. On sunny weekends the lawns fill with people having picnics, playing games and reading in the shade of the trees. Joggers circle the paths in the early morning, and in the evening couples stroll by the pond. Many parks were created in the nineteenth century, when planners realised that crowded cities needed green spaces for health and recreation.",
    "Glass blowing has changed surprisingly little over two thousand years. The craftsman gathers a glowing blob of molten glass on the end of a long hollow pipe, blows gently to form a bubble and then shapes it by turning, swinging and pressing it against wet wooden tools. The glass must be reheated again and again in the furnace while it is worked, and the finished piece is cooled slowly overnight so that it does not crack.",
    "Rush hour on the underground is an experience that many commuters would prefer to avoid. Trains arrive every two minutes, packed with people standing shoulder to shoulder, reading, listening to music or simply staring at the floor. At the busiest stations, staff guide passengers along the platforms to keep everyone moving. By ten o'clock the crowds have thinned, and the same carriages are almost empty.",
    "Cleaning out an attic often turns into a journey through the past. Under layers of dust there may be old toys, school reports, faded clothes and letters from relatives long gone. Each box seems to hold a story, and what was meant to be a quick tidy can take an entire weekend. In the end, most people keep far more than they planned to throw away, unable to part with their memories.",
    "Neighbours in the small street have organised a summer party every year for more than two decades. Tables are set up in the middle of the road, everyone brings food to share, and someone always produces a set of old speakers to play music. Children run between the houses while the adults talk late into the evening. For many residents it is the one day of the year when they get to know the newcomers.",
    "Waiting rooms are strange places where strangers sit together in silence, each absorbed in their own worries. Some people read the old magazines on the table, others stare at their screens, and a few try to start conversations that rarely last. The ticking of a clock and the occasional call of a name are often the only sounds. When your own name is finally called, the relief can be surprisingly strong.",
    # food and cooking
    "Making bread at home requires only a few ingredients: flour, water, salt and yeast. The dough must be kneaded until it becomes smooth and elastic, then left in a warm place to rise until it has doubled in size. After shaping, it rises again before going into a hot oven, and soon the whole house is filled with a wonderful smell. Many people find the slow process relaxing and deeply rewarding in its own way.",
    "Spices were once so valuable that they were traded for gold, and wars were fought over the islands where they grew. Pepper, cinnamon, cloves and nutmeg travelled thousands of kilometres by ship and caravan before reaching the kitchens of wealthy households. Today they are cheap and available in every supermarket, but they still transform simple ingredients into rich and fragrant dishes in cuisines all around the world.",
    "In many countries, the main meal of the day was traditionally eaten at midday, followed by a short rest during the hottest hours. Shops closed, streets emptied and families gathered around the table for several courses. In recent decades, longer working days and modern office hours have changed this pattern in many places, but in some towns the long lunch and the quiet afternoon still survive, especially on weekends.",
    "Cheese making began thousands of years ago, probably by accident, when milk stored in containers made from animal stomachs turned solid. Over time, people learned to control the process, adding salt, pressing the curds and ageing the cheese in cool caves or cellars. Today there are many hundreds of varieties, from soft fresh cheeses eaten within days to hard ones that are aged for several years before they are ready.",
    "A good stew needs time more than anything else. Pieces of meat or beans are browned in a heavy pot, then onions, carrots and herbs are added, along with stock or wine. The pot is covered and left to cook slowly for hours, until everything is tender and the flavours have blended together. Many cooks insist that a stew tastes even better the next day, after it has rested overnight.",
    "Weather balloons are released twice a day from hundreds of stations around the world. Each one carries a small box of instruments that measures temperature, humidity and pressure as it rises, sending the readings back by radio waves to the ground. As the balloon climbs into thinner air it expands, until it finally bursts high in the sky and the instruments float back down on a small parachute, often landing many kilometres away.",
    "Preserving fruit for the winter was once an essential task in every rural household. In late summer, kitchens were filled with steam as fruit was boiled with sugar into jams or packed into glass jars and sealed. Rows of jars were stored in cool cellars, ready to be opened on cold winter days. Many families still keep up the tradition, sharing recipes that have been passed down for generations.",
    "Rice is the main food for more than half of the people on the planet. It grows best in flooded fields, where the water keeps weeds away, and in hilly regions farmers have carved terraces into the slopes to create flat paddies. Planting and harvesting by hand is hard work, often done by whole villages together. There are thousands of varieties, differing in grain length, colour, texture and aroma.",
    # sports and games
    "Marathon runners train for months before a race, gradually increasing the distance of their long weekly runs. On the day itself, they must pace themselves carefully, since starting too fast can lead to exhaustion long before the finish. Crowds line the streets to cheer them on, offering water, fruit and encouragement. For many participants, simply finishing the course is the goal, and the time on the clock matters far less.",
    "Composting turns kitchen scraps, fallen leaves and grass cuttings into dark crumbly soil that gardeners value highly. Worms, fungi and countless bacteria do most of the work, breaking down the material over several months. A good heap needs a mixture of green and brown matter, enough air and a little moisture, and it can become surprisingly warm in the middle as the microbes multiply and feed.",
    "Swimming in open water is very different from swimming in a pool. There are no lane ropes or walls to rest on, the water may be cold and choppy, and swimmers must lift their heads regularly to check their direction. Many people find it exhilarating, especially at dawn when the surface is calm and mist rises from the lake. Safety is important, and most swimmers go in groups or with a support boat nearby.",
    "The village cricket match is a summer tradition in many places, played on a green surrounded by trees, with a small wooden pavilion where tea is served between innings. The players range from teenagers to grandfathers, and the standard of play varies widely. Spectators sit on deckchairs around the boundary, chatting and occasionally applauding a good shot. The result often matters less than the long, relaxed afternoon together.",
    "Before electric lamps, city streets were lit by oil and later by gas, and every evening lamplighters walked their routes with long poles, lighting each lamp in turn. In the morning they returned to put them out again. The arrival of gas lighting made streets far safer after dark, and shops began to stay open later. In a few old quarters, gas lamps are still lit by hand for the benefit of visitors.",
    "Card games have been played for centuries in homes, taverns and clubs around the world. Some rely mostly on luck, while others require memory, careful planning and the ability to read the other players' intentions. Families often have their own favourite games with house rules passed down over generations, and a pack of cards remains one of the simplest ways to pass a rainy afternoon with friends or relatives.",
    "Rowing requires a crew to move in perfect rhythm, with every stroke timed exactly to match the others. The rowers sit facing backwards, so they rely on the coxswain, who sits at the stern, to steer the boat and call out instructions. Training starts early in the morning, often before sunrise, when the river is calm and empty. A well-drilled crew glides across the water with remarkable speed and grace.",
    "Mountain climbing combines physical strength with careful planning and good judgement. Climbers must watch the weather, choose safe routes and know when to turn back, even if the summit is close. At high altitude the air contains less oxygen, so every step requires more effort, and many climbers spend weeks getting used to the conditions before attempting the highest peaks. Reaching the top is only half the journey.",
    # arts, music and literature
    "An orchestra may include more than a hundred musicians, each reading their own part from the score. The conductor stands in front of them, setting the tempo, shaping the volume and bringing in different sections at the right moment. Rehearsals can be long and demanding, with passages repeated many times until every detail is right. On the night of the concert, all that work comes together in a performance lasting little more than an hour.",
    "Many great novels were first published in instalments in magazines, with readers waiting eagerly each week or month for the next part. Authors often ended each section with a moment of suspense to keep their audience coming back. Some writers even changed their plots in response to letters from readers. Today the same stories are read as complete books, but traces of their original serial form can still be seen in their structure.",
    "Pottery is one of the oldest crafts, and fragments of clay pots are among the most common objects found by archaeologists. The potter shapes wet clay by hand or on a spinning wheel, lets it dry slowly and then fires it in a kiln at very high temperatures. Glazes made from minerals can add colour and shine. Each culture developed its own styles, and experts can often tell where and when a pot was made.",
    "The village choir rehearses every Wednesday evening in the old schoolroom, and its members range from teenagers to people in their eighties. Few of them can read music fluently, so the director teaches each part patiently, line by line. Twice a year they give a concert in the church, and the whole village turns out to listen. Afterwards there is always tea and cake, and the singers relax after months of practice.",
    "Street art has transformed many neighbourhoods that were once grey and neglected. Large murals cover the sides of buildings, showing portraits, landscapes and scenes from local history. Some works are commissioned by the city, while others appear overnight without permission. Visitors now come on guided walks to see them, and local artists have become well known far beyond their own streets, with some invited to paint walls in other countries.",
    "Folk tales were passed down by word of mouth long before anyone wrote them down. Each storyteller added their own details, so the same tale might exist in dozens of different versions across a region. In the nineteenth century, scholars began travelling from village to village to record these stories, preserving them in books. Many of the tales children hear today come from those collections, though often in gentler forms.",
    "Learning to play the violin is notoriously difficult at first. The instrument has no frets, so the player must find exactly the right place for each finger by ear, and drawing the bow smoothly across the strings takes months of practice. Beginners often produce squeaks and scratches that test the patience of everyone in the house. Yet with persistence, the same instrument can produce some of the most beautiful sounds in music.",
    "Theatre audiences in earlier centuries were often noisy and lively, eating, drinking and talking during performances. People in the cheapest places stood close to the stage and shouted comments at the actors, while wealthier spectators sat in boxes where they could be seen as much as they could see. Only later did it become usual for audiences to sit quietly in the dark, giving their full attention to the play.",
    "A good portrait captures more than a person's appearance; it suggests something of their character and mood. Painters in earlier times often included objects that hinted at their sitter's profession, interests or status, such as books, instruments or maps. Studying these details can reveal a great deal about the person and the period. Some portraits took months to complete, with the sitter returning to the studio again and again.",
    "Jazz grew up in the early twentieth century, combining influences from blues, ragtime, marching bands and many other traditions. Its musicians valued improvisation, creating new melodies on the spot over familiar chord patterns, so that no two performances were ever the same. From small clubs and dance halls it spread around the world, and it has influenced almost every kind of popular music that followed it.",
    # work and economy
    "Small shops on the high street have faced many challenges in recent years, from rising rents to competition from large supermarkets and online retailers. Those that survive often do so by offering something the bigger stores cannot: personal service, specialist knowledge or unusual products. Many owners know their regular customers by name and remember what they bought last time, which helps to build a loyal following over the years.",
    "In the past, many towns grew up around a single industry, such as mining, textiles or shipbuilding. Almost every family depended on it for work, and the rhythm of life followed the shifts at the mine or the mill. When those industries declined, the towns faced hard times, with shops closing and young people leaving to find work elsewhere. Some have since reinvented themselves through tourism, education or new businesses.",
    "Working from home has become common for many office workers, especially since the early twenty-twenties. Some people enjoy the freedom to organise their own day and the time saved by not commuting. Others miss the company of colleagues and find it hard to separate work from home life. Many companies now use a mixture of both arrangements, with staff coming to the office on certain days and staying at home on others.",
    "Fishing communities along the coast have always lived with uncertainty. The size of the catch varies from year to year, storms can keep boats in harbour for days, and the work itself is dangerous. In recent decades, limits on how much can be caught have been introduced to protect fish stocks, which has changed the industry considerably. Many harbours now have fewer boats than before, but those that remain are larger and better equipped.",
    "A busy restaurant kitchen during the evening service is a place of intense activity. Orders arrive one after another, and the head chef calls them out to the cooks, each responsible for a particular station. Timing is everything, since all the dishes for one table must be ready at the same moment. The pace is fast and the heat is fierce, yet the best kitchens run with remarkable calm and coordination.",
    "Apprenticeships were for centuries the main way of learning a trade. A young person would live and work with a master craftsman for several years, starting with simple tasks and gradually taking on more responsibility. At the end of the training, they might produce a special piece of work to show their skill before becoming a master themselves. Many modern training programmes still follow a similar pattern of learning by doing.",
    "Farmers' cooperatives allow small producers to share equipment, storage and transport, and to sell their goods together at better prices. Members usually have an equal say in how the cooperative is run, regardless of the size of their farm. In many rural areas these organisations have helped families stay on the land when they might otherwise have been forced to sell. Some cooperatives have grown into large and well-known brands.",
    "Markets for second-hand goods have become increasingly popular, as people look for bargains and try to reduce waste. Furniture, clothes, books and tools that might once have been thrown away now find new owners through local sales, charity shops and websites. Some collectors spend their weekends searching for rare items among the stalls, while others simply enjoy the pleasure of finding something useful at a fraction of its original price.",
    # travel and transport
    "Travelling by night train has a special charm. Passengers settle into narrow compartments with bunks, watch the lights of towns slip past the window and fall asleep to the steady rhythm of the wheels. In the morning they wake up hundreds of kilometres away, often in a different country, ready to start the day without the stress of an early flight. Several new night routes have opened in recent years.",
    "Lighthouses once guided ships along dangerous coasts, their lamps turning through the night so that sailors could recognise each one by the pattern of its flashes. Keepers lived in or beside the towers, climbing the stairs several times a night to trim the wicks and wind the mechanisms. Today almost all lighthouses are automatic, and many of the keepers' houses have been turned into holiday homes or small museums.",
    "The ferry crossing takes about two hours in good weather, and many passengers spend it on the open deck, watching seabirds follow the boat and the distant coastline slowly appear on the horizon. In winter the crossing can be rough, and the cafeteria fills with people trying to stay warm and steady. For the islanders, the ferry is a lifeline, bringing food, post and visitors from the mainland every day.",
    "Long-distance walking routes now criss-cross many countries, following old pilgrim paths, drovers' roads and coastal tracks. Walkers carry their belongings on their backs and stay each night in guesthouses, hostels or tents. Some complete a route in a single trip lasting several weeks, while others walk it in sections over many years. Along the way they meet fellow travellers, and lasting friendships are often formed.",
    "Airports are like small cities, with their own shops, restaurants, police, fire services and thousands of workers. Behind the scenes, baggage travels along kilometres of moving conveyors, fuel trucks move between the aircraft and controllers in the tower guide planes on and off the runways. At the busiest airports a plane takes off or lands every minute or two, from early morning until late at night.",
    "Before the invention of engines, travel over land depended on the strength of people and animals. Merchants walked beside heavily loaded carts, wealthy travellers rode in coaches that changed horses at inns along the way, and journeys that now take a few hours lasted several days. Roads were often muddy and full of holes, and robbers were a real danger on lonely stretches through forests and hills.",
    "The tram network in the old city was almost removed in the middle of the twentieth century, when many people thought that buses and cars were the future. A few lines survived, and in recent decades the city has extended them again, building new routes to the suburbs. The modern trams are quiet and comfortable, and the streets along their routes have become more pleasant, with fewer cars and wider pavements.",
    "Cruise ships have grown to an enormous size, with some carrying more than five thousand passengers along with a crew of over two thousand. They contain theatres, swimming pools, restaurants and even climbing walls. Supporters say they offer a relaxing way to see many places in a short time, while critics point to their environmental impact and the crowds they bring to small historic ports.",
    # health and the body
    "Sleep is essential for both body and mind. During the night, the brain sorts through the events of the day, strengthening some memories and discarding others, while the body repairs tissues and restores energy. Most adults need between seven and nine hours, though many get less. A lack of sleep affects mood, concentration and health, and over long periods it can increase the risk of many illnesses.",
    "Regular walking is one of the simplest and most effective forms of exercise. It strengthens the heart, helps control weight and improves mood, and it requires no special equipment beyond a comfortable pair of shoes. Doctors often recommend at least half an hour a day, which can be broken into shorter walks. Many people find that a walk in a park or along a river also helps them think more clearly.",
    "Mushrooms are only the visible part of a much larger organism that lives hidden in the soil or inside rotting wood. Beneath the surface, fine threads spread through the ground for great distances, breaking down dead material and sometimes linking the roots of different trees. After autumn rains, the fruiting bodies appear almost overnight, and collectors head into the forest early in the morning to find them.",
    "In very cold winters, the canals and lakes of some flat northern regions freeze thick enough to skate on, and whole towns take to the ice. Families glide along the frozen waterways between villages, stopping at small stalls that sell hot drinks and pancakes. In exceptional years a famous long-distance race is held over two hundred kilometres of natural ice, and thousands of skaters take part.",
    "Our sense of taste is closely linked to our sense of smell. When we have a heavy cold, food often seems bland because the smell receptors in our nose cannot work properly. The tongue itself detects only a few basic tastes, such as sweet, salty, sour and bitter, while most of the rich flavours we enjoy come from smells rising from the food at the back of the mouth.",
    "Bones are living tissue that is constantly being broken down and rebuilt throughout our lives. Children's bones grow quickly, while in older age they may become thinner and more fragile. Exercise, especially activities that involve carrying weight, helps keep bones strong, as does a diet with enough calcium and vitamin D. When a bone breaks, the body begins repairing it almost immediately, forming new tissue around the fracture.",
    # education
    "In many schools, pupils now learn outdoors for part of the week, studying plants, insects and weather in the school grounds or in nearby woods. Teachers say that children often concentrate better after time outside, and that lessons about nature are more memorable when pupils can see and touch what they are studying. Some schools have created ponds, vegetable plots and small orchards to support this kind of learning.",
    "Learning a second language is easier for young children, who often pick up new sounds and words with little effort. Adults, however, have advantages of their own, including a better understanding of grammar and more experience with learning strategies. Regular practice is the key at any age. Many learners find that conversations with native speakers, films and songs help far more than memorising lists of words.",
    "The university was founded more than six hundred years ago, and some of its oldest buildings are still used for teaching. Students attend lectures in halls with high wooden ceilings and study in libraries filled with ancient manuscripts as well as modern computers. Each autumn, thousands of new students arrive from around the world, and the narrow streets of the old town fill with bicycles and the sound of many languages.",
    "Homework has long been a subject of debate among parents and teachers. Some believe it helps children develop good study habits and reinforces what they learn in class, while others argue that young pupils need time to play, rest and spend with their families. Many schools now try to find a balance, setting small amounts of homework for younger children and more for older pupils preparing for examinations.",
    "Museums increasingly design exhibitions that invite visitors to touch, listen and take part rather than simply look. Children can dig for model fossils, try on historical costumes or build simple machines. Curators say that hands-on activities help people remember what they have learned and make museums more welcoming to families. At the same time, the most precious objects remain safely behind glass, protected from light and dust.",
    # animals
    "On steep hillsides above certain rivers, vines have been grown for centuries on narrow terraces held up by stone walls. The slopes are too steep for machines, so most of the work is still done by hand, from pruning in winter to the harvest in autumn. The terraces catch the sunlight reflected from the water, and growers say that this gives the grapes their particular character.",
    "Dry stone walls criss-cross the hills of many farming regions, built without any mortar from stones cleared out of the fields. A skilled waller chooses each stone carefully so that it locks firmly with its neighbours, and a well-built wall can stand for hundreds of years. The walls mark boundaries, shelter animals from the wind and provide homes for mosses, insects and small birds.",
    "In early spring, when the nights are still frosty but the days have begun to warm, sap starts to flow inside maple trees. Farmers tap the trunks with small spouts and collect the clear, slightly sweet liquid in pails or through networks of thin tubes. The sap is then boiled for hours in wide shallow pans until most of the water has evaporated, leaving a thick golden syrup. It takes about forty litres of sap to make one litre.",
    "Wolves hunt in packs, working together to track and catch animals much larger than themselves. Each pack has a territory that it defends against others, marking its borders and howling to announce its presence. In regions where wolves were once wiped out, they have sometimes been reintroduced, and their return has changed the behaviour of deer and other animals, with effects that spread through the whole landscape.",
]

